WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.456 --> 00:00:14.832
So I am here ...

00:00:15.791 --> 00:00:17.001
to recruit you ...

00:00:17.918 --> 00:00:19.712
to the liberation movement.

00:00:20.963 --> 00:00:24.633
Now don't worry, this is not the uniform
that you have to wear.

00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:26.302
(Laughter)

00:00:26.302 --> 00:00:28.804
You can come as yourself.

00:00:30.014 --> 00:00:33.517
So you're probably wondering,
"Well, what are we being liberated from?"

00:00:34.602 --> 00:00:40.357
We are being liberated
from the endless weight of data.

00:00:41.817 --> 00:00:43.527
It keeps us bound ...

00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:47.531
so that we can't move into the future,

00:00:47.531 --> 00:00:50.868
because we're so busy
being tied to the past.

00:00:52.453 --> 00:00:53.913
So how do we get free?

00:00:55.498 --> 00:00:58.292
The freedom comes from our intuition.

00:00:59.126 --> 00:01:01.295
The thing that's right here,

00:01:01.337 --> 00:01:04.006
you know, that you can't really
quite quantify.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.093
Sometimes, you can't
even really describe it.

00:01:07.635 --> 00:01:09.845
That intuition, that's the thing.

00:01:10.971 --> 00:01:14.934
Now I'm fortunate
to have had a career in marketing,

00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:19.355
where data and intuition
and creative ideas

00:01:19.396 --> 00:01:21.357
are sort of intertwined.

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:27.655
But at the beginning of my career,
I had a boss who told me

00:01:27.696 --> 00:01:32.409
that the data was going to be
extraordinarily important

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:34.120
to the success of my ideas.

00:01:34.161 --> 00:01:35.329
Right?

00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:37.039
Anchor the idea in the data,

00:01:37.081 --> 00:01:39.625
you can predict whether or not
it's going to work,

00:01:39.625 --> 00:01:42.920
and then you can see
if it actually did work, and vice versa.

00:01:44.213 --> 00:01:47.466
Well, I mean, look, I took that advice.

00:01:48.342 --> 00:01:51.220
I baked a lot my ideas
right there in the data.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.055
I was inspired, sometimes.

00:01:53.055 --> 00:01:55.724
I'd see a pattern of something
moving, and I would say,

00:01:55.724 --> 00:01:57.643
"Look, I can follow that!

00:01:57.977 --> 00:01:59.186
Maybe it'll work."

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:03.983
But the challenge was
that my ideas stopped right there.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.694
They didn't really take flight.

00:02:07.486 --> 00:02:12.491
Because, you know, the ideas are based
off of electricity, you know?

00:02:12.491 --> 00:02:14.076
Ideas, like, come sharp.

00:02:15.119 --> 00:02:18.497
They sprint here, and they go here,
and they move there.

00:02:18.539 --> 00:02:19.707
They're nonlinear.

00:02:20.708 --> 00:02:23.627
I kind of feel like
they're like daydreams.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.463
You know? Intuition gets you there.

00:02:27.006 --> 00:02:31.051
It feels like something
that is just on the horizon.

00:02:31.844 --> 00:02:34.054
And then, sometimes,
it comes into sharp focus.

00:02:34.096 --> 00:02:36.557
You wake up and you remember
exactly what it was.

00:02:37.474 --> 00:02:40.019
That's what ideas feel like.

00:02:40.895 --> 00:02:44.106
And your intuition is actually
what helps you get there.

00:02:44.148 --> 00:02:46.650
It takes it from something
that is so practical

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.028
to something that is magical.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:57.119
Now, I'm also very, very, very grateful
for a boss I had early on,

00:02:57.161 --> 00:02:58.746
who affirmed my intuition.

00:02:59.121 --> 00:03:02.625
I worked for Spike Lee
at his advertising agency.

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:05.336
This was in the early 2000s.

00:03:05.711 --> 00:03:07.213
And at the time,

00:03:07.254 --> 00:03:12.760
Pepsi had commissioned him
to create a commercial

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.055
for a campaign
that they were trying to run

00:03:16.055 --> 00:03:17.264
on the main brand.

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:21.143
Spike asked everybody in the office

00:03:21.185 --> 00:03:24.271
to come up with some thoughts
on what the talent could be

00:03:24.313 --> 00:03:26.565
that would star in the commercial.

00:03:27.775 --> 00:03:31.820
And at the time, I was
an assistant account executive.

00:03:31.862 --> 00:03:34.323
There were people who were
much more senior than I,

00:03:34.323 --> 00:03:36.408
who knew exactly what they were doing,

00:03:36.408 --> 00:03:38.702
who had come up
with a bunch of great ideas.

00:03:38.744 --> 00:03:39.912
They went to the data.

00:03:40.913 --> 00:03:44.875
They went to the Billboard charts
to look at who was at the top.

00:03:45.376 --> 00:03:48.462
They went to album sales
to see who was selling,

00:03:48.462 --> 00:03:50.631
who the public really loved.

00:03:51.465 --> 00:03:54.927
They looked at lists
that experts had put together.

00:03:54.927 --> 00:03:57.054
Who's at the top?
Who's really going to win?

00:03:57.054 --> 00:03:58.264
Who has longevity?

00:03:59.348 --> 00:04:02.393
Well, I didn't really understand
any of that data.

00:04:02.393 --> 00:04:03.394
(Laughs)

00:04:03.435 --> 00:04:04.687
I could look at it,

00:04:04.687 --> 00:04:08.482
but I didn't really
trust myself to interpret it.

00:04:08.482 --> 00:04:10.985
So I went to the thing I did know,

00:04:11.026 --> 00:04:12.152
which is MTV.

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:13.904
(Laughs)

00:04:13.946 --> 00:04:18.450
There had been a made-for-TV movie

00:04:18.492 --> 00:04:22.579
called "Carmen: A Hip Hopera," on MTV.

00:04:22.579 --> 00:04:23.747
I loved it.

00:04:23.789 --> 00:04:25.040
It starred Beyoncé.

00:04:26.083 --> 00:04:27.501
Now I thought it was magical,

00:04:27.543 --> 00:04:31.297
because, I mean, who the hell
puts hip-hop and opera together?

00:04:32.631 --> 00:04:34.216
It's fascinating.

00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:38.637
So, I put my vote on Beyoncé.

00:04:38.679 --> 00:04:40.848
Now of course, today,
everybody would be like,

00:04:40.848 --> 00:04:43.767
"Well, of course, that makes
so much sense, why wouldn't you?

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:45.561
Bet on her. Yeah, she's a winner."

00:04:46.437 --> 00:04:49.523
But at the time, the data
would tell us differently.

00:04:50.983 --> 00:04:56.322
No, there are not a lot of solo artists
that come out of girl groups

00:04:56.363 --> 00:04:57.614
and are successful.

00:04:58.157 --> 00:04:59.992
In my opinion, there is only one.

00:05:00.034 --> 00:05:01.201
(Laughs)

00:05:01.201 --> 00:05:02.369
Diana Ross.

00:05:03.454 --> 00:05:04.872
Yeah, the one.

00:05:04.913 --> 00:05:06.457
(Laughter)

00:05:06.457 --> 00:05:07.750
Beyoncé is another.

00:05:08.542 --> 00:05:11.337
So at the time, nobody was betting on her.

00:05:12.713 --> 00:05:14.965
But I'm so grateful that Spike ...

00:05:16.216 --> 00:05:19.011
affirmed my intuition
and also followed his.

00:05:19.011 --> 00:05:21.764
And I would say, today,
that we’re pretty successful.

00:05:21.764 --> 00:05:24.558
(Laughter)

00:05:25.559 --> 00:05:30.898
Now, 20 years later,
I still use my intuition, daily.

00:05:31.815 --> 00:05:34.443
I'm the chief marketing
officer at Netflix.

00:05:34.485 --> 00:05:38.781
You'd probably say that maybe
the stakes weren't as high

00:05:38.822 --> 00:05:41.158
when I was an assistant account executive,

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.869
so I could use my intuition,
and who cared.

00:05:44.495 --> 00:05:46.955
And maybe today, that's different.

00:05:46.997 --> 00:05:48.540
It’s not.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:53.462
Every day, I am charged
with looking at campaigns

00:05:53.504 --> 00:05:58.175
that are 15 seconds long,
60 seconds long, 90 seconds long,

00:05:58.217 --> 00:06:02.596
that will encourage you to watch
something that's much longer.

00:06:02.596 --> 00:06:03.764
Every day.

00:06:04.473 --> 00:06:07.393
And so I have to use
my intuition to understand

00:06:07.434 --> 00:06:10.979
whether or not something
is going to make you cry immediately,

00:06:11.021 --> 00:06:13.190
is it going to make you laugh immediately,

00:06:13.232 --> 00:06:14.942
is it going to scare you?

00:06:14.983 --> 00:06:16.777
Is it going to inspire you?

00:06:17.903 --> 00:06:20.239
And I feel like if I feel that ...

00:06:21.448 --> 00:06:23.033
then perhaps you do too.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:25.911
Now I'm not the only one.

00:06:25.953 --> 00:06:28.914
There are other people who do this.

00:06:28.914 --> 00:06:33.085
One is over 100 years ago, Henry Ford.

00:06:34.586 --> 00:06:39.133
He's attributed with saying
that if he had asked people,

00:06:39.174 --> 00:06:43.345
at the time when he was
inventing the Model T,

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:48.475
if they wanted something like that,

00:06:48.475 --> 00:06:49.476
(Laughs)

00:06:49.518 --> 00:06:52.104
they would have said
they wanted faster horses ...

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.232
instead of this new invention.

00:06:56.275 --> 00:06:58.360
I think that's pretty powerful.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.154
Or you can look at today.

00:07:00.195 --> 00:07:03.824
A more recent example,
Ed Bastian and the team at Delta,

00:07:04.324 --> 00:07:07.744
you know, based on intuition, I believe,

00:07:07.786 --> 00:07:12.708
decided that after the restrictions
of leaving that middle seat open

00:07:12.749 --> 00:07:16.503
so that those who are flying
would feel safer,

00:07:16.503 --> 00:07:20.132
with some distance in-between them,

00:07:20.132 --> 00:07:22.217
that after those restrictions were lifted,

00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:24.261
they still kept that middle seat open.

00:07:25.053 --> 00:07:27.848
Now I'm sure they were losing
millions of dollars every day

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:29.433
with that decision,

00:07:29.433 --> 00:07:31.393
but the intuition was right.

00:07:31.435 --> 00:07:33.979
Do you feel safer? Do I feel safer?

00:07:34.313 --> 00:07:36.023
What would make us feel safer?

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.442
I think that's the right thing.

00:07:39.026 --> 00:07:42.946
Now look, we can all look at 2020 and 2021

00:07:42.946 --> 00:07:47.493
and understand that, oof,
we can't really predict what happens.

00:07:47.534 --> 00:07:48.577
(Laughs)

00:07:48.577 --> 00:07:49.995
We don't really know.

00:07:49.995 --> 00:07:52.289
But what we do know is right here.

00:07:53.123 --> 00:07:56.502
What are the things
that make us feel good,

00:07:56.543 --> 00:07:58.504
the things that scare us,

00:07:58.545 --> 00:08:01.715
the things that make us feel
more connected to each other?

00:08:03.342 --> 00:08:04.593
You know, today ...

00:08:06.136 --> 00:08:07.262
is a gift.

00:08:08.388 --> 00:08:10.265
That’s why they call it the present.

00:08:10.307 --> 00:08:11.350
(Laughter)

00:08:11.391 --> 00:08:13.977
I didn't make that up,
but it's so corny and I love it.

00:08:14.019 --> 00:08:15.145
(Laughter)

00:08:16.063 --> 00:08:18.398
But my addition to it

00:08:18.398 --> 00:08:21.318
is the question: "Why would we give
the present back?"

00:08:22.486 --> 00:08:24.571
Oh, your intuition is a gift.

00:08:25.822 --> 00:08:27.282
So we have to use it.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.118
So then let's talk about you.

00:08:30.118 --> 00:08:31.703
You have your intuition.

00:08:32.996 --> 00:08:35.249
And we'll use some science in here too.

00:08:35.249 --> 00:08:36.833
We're not totally against data.

00:08:36.875 --> 00:08:37.876
(Laughs)

00:08:38.502 --> 00:08:43.257
That if you are a set up of molecules, OK?

00:08:44.925 --> 00:08:49.471
Every one of us has
a unique pattern, a unique set.

00:08:50.472 --> 00:08:53.600
How remarkable is that --
no two of us are the same.

00:08:54.226 --> 00:08:55.811
So one molecule changes,

00:08:55.852 --> 00:08:57.729
and we have a whole other being.

00:08:59.189 --> 00:09:03.235
Now if you consider that we take
ourselves as a whole --

00:09:03.235 --> 00:09:05.529
let's pretend we're one big molecule,

00:09:05.529 --> 00:09:08.365
and that over there,
the matter over there,

00:09:08.407 --> 00:09:13.203
is life and the experiences,
the services, the communities

00:09:13.203 --> 00:09:14.997
that we serve,

00:09:15.038 --> 00:09:18.292
and we enter that matter --

00:09:18.333 --> 00:09:19.459
it changes.

00:09:20.794 --> 00:09:23.380
You step out of the matter, it changes.

00:09:24.881 --> 00:09:26.800
So of course you're powerful.

00:09:27.509 --> 00:09:29.011
So why wouldn't your intuition,

00:09:29.011 --> 00:09:30.721
your own thing that you have,

00:09:30.762 --> 00:09:31.888
be powerful?

00:09:33.223 --> 00:09:34.850
I think about that all the time.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:41.773
And perhaps you don't want to take
the big, big swing just yet.

00:09:42.983 --> 00:09:45.235
You know, you can take the little steps.

00:09:45.527 --> 00:09:46.695
You can practice.

00:09:47.446 --> 00:09:48.947
I've practiced for a long time.

00:09:48.989 --> 00:09:51.283
I want to encourage you to practice too.

00:09:51.283 --> 00:09:53.785
So maybe you don't want to,
you know, bet on Beyoncé.

00:09:53.827 --> 00:09:54.828
(Laughs)

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.080
Or maybe you don't want
to create the Model T.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:00.000
Maybe you don't want to make
the call about the middle seat.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.960
So let's take it to something

00:10:02.002 --> 00:10:04.713
that may be a little bit easier,
a little closer to home.

00:10:04.713 --> 00:10:06.965
Let's pretend you're curating
a dinner party.

00:10:07.799 --> 00:10:10.344
Going to be safe about it --
we have six people over.

00:10:11.345 --> 00:10:15.265
And you're considering all the data
around who likes what.

00:10:15.599 --> 00:10:18.560
Right? There's some people who, you know,

00:10:18.602 --> 00:10:20.479
maybe somebody is a pescatarian,

00:10:20.479 --> 00:10:23.482
another person doesn't eat dairy,

00:10:23.482 --> 00:10:25.776
another hates spicy food,

00:10:25.817 --> 00:10:30.030
somebody only likes their food
really, really hot, with heat,

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.865
and you have to consider all of this.

00:10:31.907 --> 00:10:34.618
Put it all together,
think about what to make.

00:10:35.994 --> 00:10:39.915
I think you'd come up
with a really bland fish dish,

00:10:39.956 --> 00:10:41.124
ugh.

00:10:41.166 --> 00:10:42.417
Something terrible.

00:10:43.502 --> 00:10:48.590
But if you used yourself as the curator,

00:10:48.632 --> 00:10:49.841
as one with intuition --

00:10:49.883 --> 00:10:52.886
think about it, yes,
put in all the data, you know?

00:10:52.928 --> 00:10:55.430
Pescatarian, the spices, all of that.

00:10:55.972 --> 00:10:59.059
I think you would come up
with something you wanted to eat too.

00:10:59.059 --> 00:11:01.478
You know? Taking all of that
into consideration.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:04.439
It might be a catfish stew.

00:11:05.232 --> 00:11:07.359
Something really delicious,

00:11:07.359 --> 00:11:08.694
something memorable.

00:11:09.111 --> 00:11:12.906
Now there's probably going to be
one person in there who hates it.

00:11:12.948 --> 00:11:13.949
(Laughs)

00:11:13.949 --> 00:11:15.867
Guaranteed, there's always one.

00:11:15.867 --> 00:11:17.035
But that's OK.

00:11:17.994 --> 00:11:20.580
Everybody else will remember that moment,

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.249
remember that experience.

00:11:22.916 --> 00:11:24.835
It will be something exciting.

00:11:24.835 --> 00:11:26.002
So consider that.

00:11:27.879 --> 00:11:29.881
As I think about data

00:11:29.881 --> 00:11:34.594
and how we want to live
and change our world,

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:41.017
I'm reminded that sometimes,
data is the pill that we take

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:43.854
to calm our insecurity

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.274
about what we intuitively know.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:49.568
So I’m advocating

00:11:50.986 --> 00:11:54.614
that we decrease the dosage of our data.

00:11:56.116 --> 00:11:59.953
And that we increase
the implementation of our intuition.

00:12:00.787 --> 00:12:01.955
You’ll feel great,

00:12:02.748 --> 00:12:03.874
you really will ...

00:12:04.791 --> 00:12:07.169
when you're able to prove to yourself

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:13.341
that the power of your intuition
is actually accurate.

00:12:14.384 --> 00:12:15.635
Thank you so much.

00:12:15.677 --> 00:12:19.556
(Applause)

